On Tuesday 06 January 2004 23:32 Jacek Dziedzic
(jacek@__N_O_S_P_A_M__janowo.net) wrote:

> First of all, I'm getting frustrated about how quickly my
> characters
> get hungry and how hard it is to find food. Things like pears,
> pancakes or food rations seem like a blessing to me, for I often find
> myself eating tripe rations or corpses, which I guess is not too good.

Eating corpses is perfectly okay, as long as you take care to eat the
right corpses. In the early levels, I recommend jackals, newts if
you're a spellcaster (there's not much meat on a newt, but they can
give you a small power boost), hobbits and goblins.

Don't eat anything that you haven't seen alive less than ~50 turns ago.
Note: zombies and mummies are already dead, even if they're walking.

Don't eat your own species unless you're an orc or a caveperson. It's
also all right to eat humans (soldiers are human; so are Kops, though
not @; elves, though @, are not) if you're an elf, dwarf, gnome or orc.

Don't eat any kitten, housecat or large cat, little dog, dog or large
dog, unless you're a caveperson (I'm not sure about orcs here; I never
play any). You can safely eat horses, and any f or d that isn't a cat
or dog.

Be careful when eating F, j (in practice only blue jellies are good to
eat), b or P. Some F make you hallucinate, most j, b and P are acidic.
Never, never, never eat green slime (though as a beginner you probably
won't meet them).

> I also try to #pray when hungry. 

Don't. Wait until you're weak.

> Is eating things that "taste
> terrible" any good (like do they reduce hunger despite tasting bad)?

Yes; raw meat will taste terrible if you're not naturally a carnivore,
but it's still good for you.

>     Second, and most frustrating is my inability to explore the
>     dungeon,
> even on DL1 or DL2. I try to (s)earch, sometimes multiple times, but
> this wastes so much time that I usually have to worry about finding
> food quickly. I guess I'm really hopeless at searching, because even
> after quaffing the potion that reveals objects (or someting along
> these lines) when I can see where all the gold on the level is, I
> still can't collect most of
> it.

That's because most of it is embedded in rock and some of it is
underground. Don't worry about the gold; if you really want it you can
always come back later, when you have a pick-axe, and collect it.

>  I think I find hidden doors almost exclusively in obvious places
> like corridors with seemingly dead ends. 

That's indeed one of the places where hidden doors tend to be.

> Does the ability to search
> depend on the race/alignment/role? 

I'll leave that for the source divers...

> Neither my Wizard nor my Rogue are
> any good at it, anyway. 

... but I expect Rogues to be better at it. And Wizards sometimes start
with a ring of searching or a wand of secret door detection.

> What is the extent (in space) of (s)earching?

All the adjacent squares.

> Should I search on every square or every other square or... any hints?

Every other square along the wall where you suspect a hidden door will
do.

> I must be doing something wrong, since I guess I find approx 1/3 of
> the dungeon on each level (at least judging by the revealed objects),
> and hence descend to quickly.

But the revealed objects aren't always in a room; most of the gems and
gold pieces are embedded in the rock. Only if you have large expanses
of leftover space (with or without gems or gold) or other objects in
undiscovered regions, you can be sure you've missed some rooms. (Or if
you can't find the down staircase, of course)

If you have a pick-axe --play A to start with one, though that's perhaps
too hard for a newbie-- you can dig into the rock to see if there are
undiscovered rooms there.
 
>     Also, are gems of any value, except for writing Elbereth with them
> or firing them from a sling?

Most gems are glass. Real gems are valuable when identified; whether
identified or not, you can throw real gems to unicorns to increase your
luck. <http://www.steelypips.org/nethack/341/gems-341.html> is the
latest gems and luck spoiler, AFAIK, which still works for 3.4.3.
 
>     Fourth, and last question, is #sitting any good except on a
>     throne?
> (obviously I haven't found one yet).

If you're polymorphed into something that can lay eggs, you #sit to lay
an egg. If you're female when an egg laid by you hatches, the offspring
will be tame. (I like having baby silver dragons for pets)

